// ============================================================================
// ZYMBOL-LANG BENCHMARK: Reading one element — a[i], d.k, m[i][j]
//
// HLZ-012. What this measures is a SLOPE, not a time: the same 60 000 reads
// against a 100-element array and against a 4 000-element one. Reading one
// element must cost the same in both, and if it does not, the engine is copying
// the collection to hand back one of its values.
//
// It cost exactly that until v0.0.9: the tree-walker evaluated the collection
// with `eval_expr`, and for a name that is `get_variable(..).clone()`, so `a[7]`
// cloned every element of `a` first. On a 4 000-element array the read block
// below took 8.6 s against the 100-element one's 0.26 s — a 33x slope where the
// register VM's was flat.
//
// Nothing in bench/ read an element by index before this: the collection
// benchmarks go through `$>`, `$|`, `$<`, `$?` and slices, all of which consume
// the whole collection anyway and so cannot tell a copy from a read. That is
// the coverage hole the go engine fell into — a 19x19 board is 361 cells, and
// `局面[点]` sits on every path it has.
//
// Run: zymbol run bench/bench_index_read.zy
// ============================================================================

<# ./lib_time => T

>> "=== Zymbol Benchmark: Indexed reads ===" ¶

t_total = T::now()

// --- the same reads, against collections of different sizes ---------------
chico = []
@ i:1..100 { chico = chico$+ i }

grande = []
@ i:1..4000 { grande = grande$+ i }

t = T::now()
s = 0
@ _k:1..60000 { s = s + chico[7] }
>> "read_small:  n=100    sum = " s "  " T::elapsed(t) ¶

t = T::now()
s = 0
@ _k:1..60000 { s = s + grande[7] }
>> "read_large:  n=4000   sum = " s "  " T::elapsed(t) ¶

// --- through a chain: the root is a name, the rest is not ------------------
matriz = []
@ i:1..200 {
    fila = []
    @ j:1..20 { fila = fila$+ (i + j) }
    matriz = matriz$+ fila
}

t = T::now()
s = 0
@ _k:1..20000 { s = s + matriz[100][10] }
>> "read_nested: 200x20   sum = " s "  " T::elapsed(t) ¶

// --- a dictionary, by key and by dot ---------------------------------------
//
// Two sizes again, and both spellings of the read: `d["k"]` goes through
// `eval_index` and `d.k` through `eval_member_access`, which had the same copy
// and needed the same fix. On 300 keys the dot alone was 0.46 s against the
// bracket's 0.045 s while only one of the two was fixed.
chica = #(fin: 7)
@ i:1..8 { clave = "k" i
    chica[clave]$~ i }

ancha = #(fin: 7)
@ i:1..300 { clave = "k" i
    ancha[clave]$~ i }

t = T::now()
s = 0
@ _k:1..40000 { s = s + chica["fin"] }
>> "key_small:   9 keys   sum = " s "  " T::elapsed(t) ¶

t = T::now()
s = 0
@ _k:1..40000 { s = s + ancha["fin"] }
>> "key_large:   301 keys sum = " s "  " T::elapsed(t) ¶

t = T::now()
s = 0
@ _k:1..40000 { s = s + chica.fin }
>> "dot_small:   9 keys   sum = " s "  " T::elapsed(t) ¶

t = T::now()
s = 0
@ _k:1..40000 { s = s + ancha.fin }
>> "dot_large:   301 keys sum = " s "  " T::elapsed(t) ¶

// --- a read inside a function, which is where the go engine does its reads --
//
// This block is NOT measuring the read. It measures what it costs to hand the
// collection over, which is a second copy in the same engine and was found by
// this very line: the tree-walker cloned the whole array into the parameter on
// every call — 2.165 s here against the register VM's 3 ms. Closing it (HLZ-014,
// `Value` shares its aggregates behind an `Rc` and copies on write) brought this
// line to 0.009 s. It stays, because a benchmark that only covers what is
// already fast finds nothing.
lee(tablero, punto) { <~ tablero[punto] }

t = T::now()
s = 0
@ _k:1..5000 { s = s + lee(grande, 7) }
>> "read_in_fn:  n=4000   sum = " s "  " T::elapsed(t) ¶

>> "=== Done (" T::total(t_total) ") ===" ¶
